## Appendix A Relativistic Kinematics

In this appendix, aspects of relativistic kinematics relevant to heavy ion physics and resonance analysis are reviewed. The purpose of this appendix is to provide an introduction for the reader who is new to heavy ion physics, and to set forth the notations and conventions used in this thesis. The following derivations use the convention in which \(\hbar=c=1\). The following conversions are useful: \(\hbar c=197.3\) MeV-fm and \((\hbar c)^{2}=0.3894\)\((\mbox{GeV})^{2}\)-mb.

### Lorentz Transformations

The energy \(E\) and 3-momentum \({\bf p}\) of a particle of mass \(m\) form the 4-vector \(p=(E,{\bf p})\), whose square \(p^{2}\equiv E^{2}-|{\bf p}|^{2}=m^{2}\). The velocity of the particle is \(\beta={\bf p}/E\). The energy and momentum \((E^{*},\,{\bf p}^{*})\) viewed from a frame moving with a velocity \(\beta_{f}\) are given by

\[\left(\begin{array}{c}E^{*}\\ p_{\parallel}^{*}\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}\gamma_{f}&- \gamma_{f}\beta_{f}\\ -\gamma_{f}\beta_{f}&\gamma_{f}\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{c}E\\ p_{\parallel}\end{array}\right)\,\ p_{t}^{*}=p_{t}\] (A.1)

where \(\gamma_{f}=1/\sqrt{1-\beta_{f}^{2}}\) and \(p_{t}(p_{\parallel})\) are the components of \({\bf p}\) perpendicular (parallel) to \(\beta_{f}\). Other 4-vectors, such as the space-time coordinates of events transform in the same manner. The scalar product of two 4-momenta \(p_{1}\cdot p_{2}=E_{1}E_{2}-{\bf p}_{1}\cdot{\bf p}_{2}\) is invariant (frame independent).

### 11 Kinematic Variables

We consider collision systems with two bodies (particles or nuclei) in the initial state and define the \(z\)-axis to coincide with the axis of collision. For the purposes of presenting single-particle differential multiplicities, it is convenient to describe particle trajectories using kinematic variables which are either Lorentz invariant or transform trivially under Lorentz boosts along this axis.

The momentum components \(p_{x}\) and \(p_{y}\) are unchanged by a boost along \(z\) so we define and use the transverse momentum of a particle,

\[p_{t}=\sqrt{p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}} \tag{110}\]

as one such variable. The transverse mass (or transverse energy) of a particle with mass \(m\) is defined as

\[m_{t}=\sqrt{p_{t}^{2}+m^{2}} \tag{111}\]

such that the transverse kinetic energy of the particle is \(m_{t}-m\).

The longitudinal variable most commonly used is rapidity,

\[y=\frac{1}{2}\ln\;\left(\frac{E+p_{z}}{E-p_{z}}\right) \tag{112}\]

which has the advantage of being additive under Lorentz transformations along \(z\). This means that under Lorentz transformations along \(z\), differences in rapidity, \(dy\), are invariant and rapidity spectra, \(dN/dy\), translate in \(y\) while their shapes are preserved. The expression for rapidity may also be written as

\[y=\;\ln\;\left(\frac{E+p_{z}}{m_{t}}\right). \tag{113}\]

From the above definitions, the relations

\[p_{z}=m_{t}\;\mbox{sinh}\;y \tag{114}\] \[E=m_{t}\;\mbox{cosh}\;y \tag{115}\]are obtained. Dividing these, we have

\[\beta_{z}=\ \tanh\ y\] (A.8)

which is the longitudinal component of the velocity of a particle of rapidity \(y\) in the lab. Since rapidity is additive under Lorentz transformations, this suggests a form for the rapidity transformation corresponding to a boost along the \(z\)-axis. If a particle has a rapidity \(y\) in the lab and we want to know its rapidity \(y^{\prime}\) in a system which has velocity \(\beta_{z}\) relative to the lab, then:

\[y^{\prime}=y-\tanh^{-1}\beta_{z}.\] (A.9)

A related quantity is the pseudo-rapidity, \(\eta\). To obtain the expression for \(\eta\), we first rewrite Equation A.4 as

\[y=\frac{1}{2}\ln\ \left(\frac{1+\beta\ \cos\theta}{1-\beta\ \cos\theta}\right)\] (A.10)

and taking the limit of Equation A.10 as \(\beta\to 1\):

\[\eta=-{\rm ln}\ {\rm tan}\ \left(\frac{\theta}{2}\right).\] (A.11)

For particles with \(\beta\approx 1,\ \eta\approx y,\) while for massless particles, \(\eta=y\). Note that a particle's \(\eta\) only depends on its angle of emission relative to the beam axis.

